The wind on the Isle of Solitude did not merely blow; it screamed. It tore at the stone walls of the lighthouse, a constant, rhythmic reminder that Elias Thorne was alone. For thirty years, he had been the keeper of the Beacon, a solitary figure in a world of gray waves and white foam. His life was measured in the rotation of the lens and the changing of the tides. He spoke to the gulls, he spoke to the rusted gears, but he did not speak to people.

Until the Tuesday the mail boat arrived with a single, cream-colored envelope.

It was not the usual government correspondence regarding fuel or maintenance. The paper was thick, textured, and smelled faintly of lavender and old paper. There was no return address, only a name written in elegant, looping script: *Elias Thorne, Keeper of the Light.*

Elias’s hands trembled as he broke the seal. He had expected a bill, or perhaps a notice of retirement. Instead, he found a letter from a woman named Clara.

*Dear Mr. Thorne,*

*I am writing to you from a small cottage in Vermont, a place where the snow falls softly and the silence is heavy. I am a historian
